     EXPLANATION OF OPTION
 
     Use this option to manually initiate reconstruction of data on a 
     failed drive.  You need to manually initiate reconstruction if 
     you have disabled the "Auto-detection Of Drive Replacement And 
     Reconstruction" option using acf.
 
     NOTE:  A procedure for reconstructing drive data is included in 
            the help screens following the explanation of option.
 
     WHAT "RECONSTRUCT" MEANS:  Reconstructing data on a drive causes the 
     array controller to do the following, in order:
 
          - Sense that there is a new drive in the array,
          - Change the drive status to "Replace" (4),
          - Copy special array controller files to the new drive,
          - Recalculate data and parity from the data and parity
            on the other drives,
          - Write the recalculated data and parity to the new drive,
          - Change the drive status to "Optimal" (0).
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     WHEN TO RECONSTRUCT DRIVE DATA:  When you replace a drive, the array 
     controller AUTOMATICALLY starts reconstructing the drive data (unless 
     you have disabled the "Auto-detection Of Drive Replacement And 
     Reconstruction" option using acf).  If you replace a drive and 
     reconstruction does NOT start automatically, manually initiate 
     reconstruction using this option.
 
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  Remember, a RAID 0 array does not have parity so 
                      you can NOT recover the data by reconstructing 
                      it.  You must restore the RAID 0 array to a 
                      working state by formatting the logical unit, 
                      then copying the data from the back-up media 
                      file-by-file (see the "Format Logical Unit" 
                      option in the logical unit window).
 
 
     ABOUT FORMATTING A DRIVE 
 
     Most of the time, a drive is pre-formatted, so the array controller 
     does not format it again.  However, you may want to format a drive 
     that is already in the logical unit if you suspect that the drive is 
     still good.
                                (CONTINUED)
     First, to force a drive format, you must disable the "Auto-detection 
     Of Drive Replacement And Reconstruction" option (using acf).  Then, 
     replace the drive, and reconstruction will NOT automatically start.  
     Next, select the "Reconstruct" drive data option in the drive window 
     to manually initiate reconstruction, and the Array Configuration 
     Editor gives you the choice to format the drive.  Press "y" to format 
     the drive.  While the drive is formatting, the drive status is 
     "Formatting" (8).
 
 
     PROCEDURE -- RECONSTRUCTING DRIVE DATA
 
     NOTE:  You can NOT restore a RAID 0 array by reconstructing it.  
            However, if you want to force a drive format, use the 
            "Reconstruct"  option. 
 
     1.   Select a drive by pressing the arrow keys.
 
     2.   Press "r" to reconstruct the data on the drive.
 
     3.   Press "n" to continue with the reconstruction,
          press "y" to force a drive format, or press "q" to quit.
 
     NOTE:  A drive format takes approximately 10 - 15 minutes
            depending on the size of the drive.
 
     4.   Monitor the reconstruction progress by periodically
          checking the percent of reconstruction complete, 
          the LUN status, and the drive's status.
 
     5.   Monitor array performance, and modify the reconstruction
          rate (delay interval and blocks per delay interval) if
          necessary.
